# Tessera stdlib: the hosted memory managers' tables
#
# On a target with an operating system the standard library comes with three tables built from Standard/Os routines:
# DEFAULT_HEAP (the C library's heap, behind the checked heap's checks when the heap check is on), DEFAULT_STACKS
# (whole pages with a guard page below each stack), and make_c_heap_memory() (plain malloc, realloc, and free). Their
# routines live in Standard/Os (Os/Heap.te, Os/Stack.te, Os/Pages.te). The tables are built here, where the table's
# fields are. On a target without one none of this is there.

module Standard/Alloc

### The hosted default allocator, ready before `start` runs: the C library's heap, with the checked heap's checks when
### the heap check is on. The collections' and {@[T]}'s `construct` without an allocator draw from it, and it can
### be passed wherever an `@MemoryManager` goes. It holds no state, so threads share it. Its page routines are the
### operating system's.
@target(os: not "none")
@heap_check("off")
global DEFAULT_HEAP: @MemoryManager <- (alloc_fn: Standard/Os/plain_heap_alloc_fn.to[Callable](), realloc_fn: Standard/Os/plain_heap_realloc_fn.to[Callable](), free_fn: Standard/Os/plain_heap_free_fn.to[Callable](), reserve_fn: Standard/Os/os_reserve_fn.to[Callable](), commit_fn: Standard/Os/os_commit_fn.to[Callable](), release_fn: Standard/Os/os_release_fn.to[Callable](), protect_fn: Standard/Os/os_protect_fn.to[Callable](), state: .hollow())
### The hosted default allocator with the heap check on: the C library's heap behind the checked heap's checks, which
### crash on a block freed twice.
@target(os: not "none")
@heap_check("on")
global DEFAULT_HEAP: @MemoryManager <- (alloc_fn: Standard/Os/checked_heap_alloc_fn.to[Callable](), realloc_fn: Standard/Os/checked_heap_realloc_fn.to[Callable](), free_fn: Standard/Os/checked_heap_free_fn.to[Callable](), reserve_fn: Standard/Os/os_reserve_fn.to[Callable](), commit_fn: Standard/Os/os_commit_fn.to[Callable](), release_fn: Standard/Os/os_release_fn.to[Callable](), protect_fn: Standard/Os/os_protect_fn.to[Callable](), state: .hollow())

### The hosted stack allocator, ready before `start` runs: whole pages, with a guard page below each stack. Each
### thread's FIBERS takes its fibers' stacks from it, and `make_stack_memory()` gives the same one.
@target(os: not "none")
global DEFAULT_STACKS: @MemoryManager <- (alloc_fn: Standard/Os/stack_alloc_fn.to[Callable](), realloc_fn: Standard/Os/stack_realloc_fn.to[Callable](), free_fn: Standard/Os/stack_free_fn.to[Callable](), reserve_fn: Standard/Os/os_reserve_fn.to[Callable](), commit_fn: Standard/Os/os_commit_fn.to[Callable](), release_fn: Standard/Os/os_release_fn.to[Callable](), protect_fn: Standard/Os/os_protect_fn.to[Callable](), state: .hollow())

### An {MemoryManager} that is the C library's malloc, realloc, and free in every build, with nothing in front of the
### blocks: for memory that C code frees or reallocates, or that comes from C's malloc and is freed here. An alignment
### past malloc's own comes from posix_memalign (still freed with free), and on Windows, whose free can't take such a
### block, it crashes. Its page routines are the operating system's.
### :returns: The allocator, which holds no state and needs nothing to free.
@target(os: not "none")
routine make_c_heap_memory() -> MemoryManager
    alloc: MemoryManager = MemoryManager(alloc_fn: Standard/Os/c_heap_alloc_fn.to[Callable](),
        realloc_fn: Standard/Os/c_heap_realloc_fn.to[Callable](),
        free_fn: Standard/Os/c_heap_free_fn.to[Callable](),
        reserve_fn: Standard/Os/os_reserve_fn.to[Callable](),
        commit_fn: Standard/Os/os_commit_fn.to[Callable](),
        release_fn: Standard/Os/os_release_fn.to[Callable](),
        protect_fn: Standard/Os/os_protect_fn.to[Callable](), state: .hollow())
    return alloc
